  m_Name: bubble_witch
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: bubble_witch
  Title: Bubble Witch
  Author: Tilly
  Color: {r: 0.6, g: 0.85, b: 0.95, a: 1}
  Hint: Atop a sea stack, halfway across to Tidewater
  Pages:
  - 'I was a much younger turtle when I knew Pearl, so you will have to trust my
    memory. Turtles have very good memories. It is one of the few advantages of being
    slow.


    Pearl lived on the Tidewater shore with her grandmother, in a cottage
    made from an upturned boat. She was the smallest witch anyone could remember.'
  - 'On their ninth birthdays the young witches of Tidewater were given their first
    brooms. Pearl was given hers, and climbed on, and the broom tipped her gently
    off into a rock pool.


    She tried again the next day, and the day after.
    The broom was very sorry about it, but she was simply too small to steer. The
    crabs began saving her a spot.'
  - 'So Pearl stopped trying to fly, and started blowing bubbles instead.


    She
    made her soap from seafoam and kelp and one secret ingredient she never told
    anyone, not even me. Her bubbles didn''t pop when you poked them. They wobbled.
    They held things.


    First a feather, then a shell, then a very surprised
    crab, and then, one breezy afternoon, Pearl herself.'
  - 'The other young witches laughed at her, though not unkindly. A girl drifting
    along the beach in a bubble is a funny thing to see.


    Pearl laughed too.
    She was having a lovely time. She floated wherever the wind went, and I swam
    along underneath, and we talked about everything, the way you only can with a
    friend who is going the same way as you.'
  - 'Then came the autumn of the great storm.


    It blew in at dusk, black and
    roaring, and it blew out the lamp on the old lighthouse rock. Out past the reef
    a fishing boat was coming home. Without the light it was lost among the sea stacks,
    turning this way and that while the waves grew taller.'
  - 'The grown witches ran down to the beach with their brooms, but the wind tore
    at them and threw them back onto the sand. Nobody could fly in such a gale.


    "A
    broom is a stick," said Pearl''s grandmother grimly. "And the wind always wins
    against a stick."


    Pearl was already unscrewing her soap jar.'
  - 'She took the deepest breath of her life, and blew.


    The bubble grew bigger
    than a barrel, bigger than a boat, bigger than her grandmother''s cottage. She
    put her lantern inside it and climbed in after it, and the wind snatched her
    up and carried her out over the waves.


    A bubble doesn''t fight the wind,
    you see. It goes with it.'
  - 'I swam after her as fast as a young turtle can, which was not nearly fast enough,
    so I watched it all from the water.


    The bubble glowed gold with lantern
    light, and silver whenever the lightning flashed. It rolled over the reef and
    bobbed among the sea stacks, up high where the sailors could see it.


    And
    the sailors saw it. And they followed.'
  - 'The boat came safely up onto the beach just before midnight, and the whole village
    was there to haul it in.


    Then Pearl''s bubble drifted down over our heads
    and popped, and it rained warm sea-spray and laughter on everyone.


    The
    fishermen said afterwards that it tasted faintly of kelp, and of something else
    they could never quite name.'
  - 'Pearl never did learn to ride a broom. She never needed to.


    She is long
    gone now, and I am an old turtle. But on calm nights, if you look out from Tidewater,
    you can still see little bubbles rising over the water, each with a tiny light
    inside.


    The village children blow them for her, to light the way home.'
